---
title: Pressure-cooker risotto
description: Lesson 8.3. Raising the boiling point of water, and what that buys you.
servings: 4
time: 30 minutes
tags: [cooking-course, mastering-the-machines]
lesson: "8.3"
technique: pressure cooking
status: draft
---

> Technique: pressure cooking. A sealed pot traps steam, the pressure
> rises, and under pressure water boils at a higher temperature. Food
> in it cooks faster for that reason alone. You cannot see, stir or
> taste while it works, so the amounts and the time have to be right
> before the lid goes on.

Heat @olive oil{1%tbsp} and half of the @butter{40%g} in the open #pressure cooker{} over medium heat (or on its sauté setting). Add @onion{1}(finely chopped) and cook for ~{5%minutes}, until soft.

Add @risotto rice{300%g}(arborio or carnaroli) and stir for ~{2%minutes}. Pour in @white wine{100%ml} and stir until it has almost gone.

Add @stock{750%ml}(chicken or vegetable, hot) and @salt{1/2%tsp}. Check that the pot is no more than two-thirds full. Lock the lid, bring the cooker to high pressure, and cook for ~{6%minutes}.

> Science: at sea level water boils at 100 °C and can get no hotter.
> At high pressure, 15 psi above atmospheric, it boils at about
> 121 °C; the weakest cooker in one test reached only 110 °C, about
> 6 psi. Those extra twenty degrees are the whole machine: under pressure, risotto
> needs 6 minutes and dried beans about 10. Look up: boiling point,
> vapour pressure, phase diagram of water.
-- source: Lisa McManus, "The Best Stovetop Pressure Cookers", America's Test Kitchen, 2025-11-10, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/equipment-reviews/1398-stovetop-pressure-cookers ("raising the boiling point from 212 to 250 degrees"; "Risotto needs just 6 minutes under pressure")

> Why the liquid and the fill line matter: a pressure cooker needs
> liquid to make the steam that cooks the food, and it must never be
> more than two-thirds full. Follow the lines marked inside your pot.
-- source: Lisa McManus, "The Best Stovetop Pressure Cookers", America's Test Kitchen, 2025-11-10, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/equipment-reviews/1398-stovetop-pressure-cookers

Release the pressure at once, following your cooker's instructions and keeping your hands and face away from the steam. Open the lid when the pressure has fully dropped.

> Why a quick release here: letting the pressure fall on its own keeps
> cooking the food gently as it drops, which suits a stew. Rice would
> overcook, so the pressure is let out straight away. The two methods
> are not interchangeable.
-- source: Sam Block, "I Just Got an Instant Pot. Now What?", America's Test Kitchen, 2022-01-10, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/4237-i-just-got-an-instant-pot-now-what

Stir the rice hard for ~{1%minute}. Stir in the rest of the butter and @parmesan{50%g}(finely grated). Taste and adjust the salt.

> If it goes wrong: you cannot test food under pressure, so sometimes
> it comes out slightly underdone. Leave the lid off, set the pot over
> medium heat, add a splash of stock and simmer until it is right.
-- source: Sam Block, "I Just Got an Instant Pot. Now What?", America's Test Kitchen, 2022-01-10, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/4237-i-just-got-an-instant-pot-now-what

> Next time, without the recipe: enough liquid to make steam, never
> more than two-thirds full, the right time for the food, and the
> right release: quick for things that overcook, natural for things
> that should finish cooking gently.
